00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:20.225
Uh, your interest in languages, is this
as a result of you traveling and being...

00:03:20.565 --> 00:03:24.015
because the average person from Kansas
City, let's say probably doesn't see a

00:03:24.015 --> 00:03:25.565
tremendous need for learning languages.

00:03:25.585 --> 00:03:25.815
Yeah.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:30.175
So what was it about your
background that made you interested?

00:03:30.755 --> 00:03:32.820
The traveling is not a part of it.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.650
Everyone assumes that because, uh,
I traveled around, I learn all these

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.790
languages when I was in the country.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.110
Nothing could be further from the truth.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:40.980
Of course.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:41.580
Yes.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.820
When I played in Croatia, when I played
in Denmark, I did learn those languages

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.500
there, but, uh, I spent three or four
days in Italy and I speak Italian.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.690
Uh, I learned French before ever living
or playing in a French speaking country.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.390
Um, German is the same.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.350
The catalyst for my language
learning is my mother, uh, who

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.000
is unfortunately passed away.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.060
But she decided at a young age
that it would be better for me to

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.060
come home and not watch, I don't
know, Pokemon or whatever it was

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.920
that I wanted to watch as a kid.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.900
And she said, Nope, you're watching this.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.330
And this was comprehensible input,
as it turns out, uh, for kids to

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.810
learn Spanish as a second language.

00:04:20.325 --> 00:04:24.555
And so I, without knowing it by the time
I was around somewhere between 10 and

00:04:24.735 --> 00:04:28.035
13, had a pretty good grip on Spanish.

00:04:28.335 --> 00:04:33.945
And, uh, that got me to realize that I
had learned the language without much

00:04:33.945 --> 00:04:37.395
effort at all, as you know, from this,
uh, the, the method and the way that, you

00:04:37.395 --> 00:04:39.164
know, you guys do do things over at LingQ.

00:04:39.765 --> 00:04:42.225
You're not really forcing too
much, you know, you don't stress

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:43.695
people to speak and do all this.

00:04:43.695 --> 00:04:46.245
So, so that was a huge factor.

00:04:46.245 --> 00:04:48.525
And then the book I read the
book The Count of Monte Cristo

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.520
and that basically sent me...

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.430
have you read it, read
that book by chance?

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.090
A long time ago.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:55.469
In English.

00:04:55.469 --> 00:04:55.900
Yeah.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:56.330
Sure.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.719
Well, the character just struck me as a
16 year old that he had this ability to

00:05:00.719 --> 00:05:03.719
travel around the world that he took all
this time and he learned these languages

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:06.120
while he was in prison and all this stuff.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.310
And then he had this ability to
just travel and do everything.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:14.895
I really, it struck me and that's,
that's the real reason why I can do this.

00:05:15.195 --> 00:05:17.685
I'd like to get back to the whole
subject of motivation because it's

00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:19.065
such a big part of language learning.

00:05:19.065 --> 00:05:22.425
But first, uh, I'm curious to know,
you know, it's been more than a

00:05:22.425 --> 00:05:26.295
year since we last spoke and you've
now launched your YouTube channel.

00:05:26.295 --> 00:05:28.335
You've got some videos
that have gone viral.

00:05:28.365 --> 00:05:30.215
Can you update us on
what you've been doing?

00:05:30.215 --> 00:05:30.475
Yeah.

00:05:30.475 --> 00:05:34.525
So, uh, we, we stopped I think it was
at the end, it might've even been just

00:05:34.525 --> 00:05:41.245
off air in the last, uh, you asked, you
know ... and I said, uh, in English, "no",

00:05:41.725 --> 00:05:48.835
because I just got into Sweden and, uh, so
yeah, I, uh, I learned Swedish, you know,

00:05:48.925 --> 00:05:53.545
uh, at a comfortable level now that I can
communicate and now it's been, been great.

00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:57.385
Uh, you know, the motivation and it's
so interesting for all the people

00:05:57.385 --> 00:06:02.970
out there that are trying to learn
it's it's regardless of the language.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.570
And it's just something that I just, I
like to hammer into people who are here

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:11.010
that have just started their language
journey or are about to, you will

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.450
be super motivated at the beginning.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.240
You will gain, uh, quite
a bit at the beginning.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:15.740
Thanks to...

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.010
you will hit a dip.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:18.660
It is guaranteed.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.160
You might not hit it in 10 days.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.350
You might not hit it in
a week, but it will come.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:23.640
You will stall at some point.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.530
And you've just got to push through that.

00:06:26.414 --> 00:06:28.094
Uh, you might even need to take a break.

00:06:28.185 --> 00:06:31.395
Uh, I'm not saying to quit, uh, you
know, if you've lost all passion

00:06:31.395 --> 00:06:33.825
for it, then it might not have been
the best choice, but if you're still

00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:37.664
interested in it, that dip part right
there is the, is the turning point.

00:06:37.664 --> 00:06:40.424
Because if you can get through the
dip and stick to it and keep your

00:06:40.424 --> 00:06:43.934
passive listening up and a little
active listening and a little bit

00:06:43.934 --> 00:06:50.984
active speaking, stuff like that, you
generally start this exponential jump up.

00:06:51.434 --> 00:06:54.235
Uh, if you know, if you're
following the method, correctly.

00:06:54.599 --> 00:06:58.890
So, um, yeah, you know, uh, I
would say just to be aware of

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:03.150
that, because motivation will wane,
uh, you know, when you pop into a

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.090
conversation that you feel as, oh
my God, I can't understand anything.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:07.320
What am I doing with my life?

00:07:07.469 --> 00:07:07.830
You know?

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:12.450
So then, so, uh, and so you're
motivated obviously to learn languages.

00:07:12.719 --> 00:07:15.150
You're also motivated to
help people learn languages.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:19.530
I can sense your enthusiasm, passion,
and so forth and how, oh, so what

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.310
are you actually, what are the kinds
of activities that you have gotten.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.109
yourself involved in?

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:25.500
In language learning?

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:27.240
Hmm.

00:07:27.299 --> 00:07:28.169
Oh gosh.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:28.510
Yeah.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.170
You do said something
about you're on YouTube...

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:30.820
oh, gosh.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:31.200
Yes.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.419
So, uh, so right now we filmed...

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:35.580
Goluremi Languages.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:36.030
Yes.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:36.930
Go check it out.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.200
So Goluremi is the name of our, obviously
we have a, we have a rather large,

00:07:40.229 --> 00:07:44.985
uh, soccer, YouTube channel and soccer
company named Goluremi, my middle

00:07:44.985 --> 00:07:49.055
name is Oluremi, and we just put the
word G for goal the Spanish word goal.

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:50.125
We mashed that together.

00:07:50.125 --> 00:07:50.784
Goal!

00:07:50.784 --> 00:07:51.315
Exactly.

00:07:51.945 --> 00:07:57.675
And, um, yeah, so we have filmed
videos, uh, language videos,

00:07:57.675 --> 00:08:00.045
in I believe Sweden in Croatia.

00:08:00.075 --> 00:08:05.685
We just did one in New York where we
spoke to, uh, we went to Little Italy

00:08:06.015 --> 00:08:09.735
and just wanted to find out if the people
there could speak Italian and that video,

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.979
it's been popped all over places and
we're going to, we've just been asked

00:08:13.979 --> 00:08:18.510
to be a part of a documentary now from,
from that video and all this stuff.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:23.190
So, uh, you know, we've had some, some
guys and that's obviously our goal

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.700
is to, is to reach people like you
as well to kind of give people a good

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.140
base of language learning understanding
to tackle it from different ways.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.240
I mean, I think one of the things
that you see in this is that many of

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.740
the successful polyglots, uh, like
you, they have certain different

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.569
methods, but they, they all have a few
things that are just, they have to...

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:49.230
Luca Lampariello uses this,
but Steve also uses this.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.890
They might differ on a few little
things, but it's just clear that

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:57.750
there's a pattern there and we want
to kind of point that out for people.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:01.020
So that's, that's the goal of the channel
and that's what you'll find there.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.800
And that's kind of just, what we've
been up to is, is, is, uh, speaking

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.574
to people in the, in these different
languages, shocking them, doing some

00:09:07.574 --> 00:09:10.665
of these old videos that you've seen
online, where you kind of shock people.

00:09:10.665 --> 00:09:11.415
Those are always fun.

00:09:11.415 --> 00:09:12.194
The reaction's great.

00:09:12.255 --> 00:09:16.575
And then the other goal is to simply
educate people on the best methods,

00:09:16.605 --> 00:09:19.815
because it is truly, probably still
plural, even though there's a lot of

00:09:19.815 --> 00:09:23.655
similarities and, uh, you know, making
it simple for people to do that.

00:09:23.655 --> 00:09:23.985
That's it.

00:09:23.985 --> 00:09:24.675
That's what we're about.

00:09:25.145 --> 00:09:26.355
You know, you mentioned the dip.

00:09:26.385 --> 00:09:30.555
That's interesting because one of the
reasons for that dip is that when we

00:09:30.555 --> 00:09:34.905
start into a new language, we achieve
a fair amount of success initially from

00:09:35.025 --> 00:09:38.385
knowing nothing about the language,
not understanding anything, all of a

00:09:38.385 --> 00:09:40.095
sudden we can understand something.

00:09:40.095 --> 00:09:42.465
We can say something we're
very impressed with ourselves.

00:09:43.319 --> 00:09:47.400
But then the reality is that a
word frequency declines very, very

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.480
quickly so that, uh, the initial
few months we're meeting the

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:52.770
same words over and over again.

00:09:53.069 --> 00:09:57.810
And then suddenly as we start to hit
words that don't appear that often it's

00:09:57.810 --> 00:09:59.459
harder and harder to remember them.

00:09:59.939 --> 00:10:04.170
And that's where it's, I think so
important to get into content of interest.

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:07.230
You can actually get to something
that you're actually interested

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.850
in hearing about it, reading it.

00:10:09.645 --> 00:10:13.365
Then that sort of the interesting nature
of the content will keep you going,

00:10:13.815 --> 00:10:18.705
because I have not found that, like after
the, I consider it more of a plateau, not

00:10:18.705 --> 00:10:22.035
like you have a sudden dip and then all
of a sudden you take off again and it, you

00:10:22.035 --> 00:10:24.595
settle into a sort of a, a long distance.

00:10:24.615 --> 00:10:28.515
You start off sprinting then pretty soon
you realize this is a long distance.

00:10:28.695 --> 00:10:29.805
It's a 50 kilometer.

00:10:29.835 --> 00:10:32.595
This is the 90 kilometer
Vasaloppet in Sweden.

00:10:32.895 --> 00:10:35.535
It's a long road anyway.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.840
But I agree with you that the
successful language learners were...

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.620
polyglot is a bit intimidating.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.760
Most English speakers don't even know
the word, uh, but successful language

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.440
learners have many, many things in common.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.680
What are some of the main things
in common that you have found?

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.020
It's a really good question.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.200
So what we have found so far,
and obviously we haven't made the

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.750
exhaustive, uh, list yet, but what...

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:05.880
it's clear a good understanding of
why they're starting to learn the

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:10.199
language they chose, even if they
want to learn many languages they

00:11:10.199 --> 00:11:13.439
generally, the successful ones,
the ones that, and by successful,

00:11:13.439 --> 00:11:15.180
I mean that they are relative...

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.589
a relatively short amount of time
is used to learn the language: one

00:11:19.589 --> 00:11:23.730
year, six months, uh, you know, this,
something like this, not like 10 years

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:28.890
to learn one, one new language, uh,
they choose, they choose that language

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.845
out of, out of an absolute huge desire.

00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:35.835
They focus then on one
language for the most part.

00:11:35.835 --> 00:11:39.735
Now it's not that most polyglots do choose
uh, don't, don't study more than one

00:11:39.735 --> 00:11:44.475
language, but what tends to happen is, uh,
one becomes more of a focus, one language

00:11:44.475 --> 00:11:49.665
kind of rules out in the brain, uh, you
know, and, uh, it, it, it seems that

00:11:50.565 --> 00:11:53.085
they have a main, they have a main focus.

00:11:53.085 --> 00:11:55.005
It, uh, you know, you might
end up dreaming in this

00:11:55.005 --> 00:11:56.125
and doing things like that.

00:11:56.635 --> 00:11:57.615
The other is the frequency.

00:11:57.615 --> 00:12:01.454
Like you, we've kind of touched on, but
it's every day, regardless of if it's

00:12:01.635 --> 00:12:05.655
five minutes and people just cannot
quite yet understand the importance of

00:12:05.655 --> 00:12:09.645
this, but the exposure that you have
in your mother tongue is enormous.

00:12:09.735 --> 00:12:11.625
And you got it every day when
you were a kid, whether you

00:12:11.625 --> 00:12:14.175
liked it or not, uh, because you
didn't control your environment.

00:12:14.564 --> 00:12:18.235
And if you're looking to learn
another, uh, language you

00:12:18.235 --> 00:12:19.760
cannot have these massive gaps.

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:22.790
That's something that all polyglots
has do not have in their success.

00:12:22.969 --> 00:12:28.610
They do not have like a six to seven
day, you know, just to stop in the

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.290
middle of their, their, their learning.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.270
It's a constant every day thing.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.510
Uh, comprehensible input as
we've kind of spoken about.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:40.310
And for the people that Steve can probably
explain it much better than I can, uh, but

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:47.260
comprehensible input, uh, is a...And the
way that I describe it as most polyglots,

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:51.220
if they don't know about it early
on, they will stumble on and find it.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.890
They will stumble their way to it,
through all the methods, maybe the

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:58.510
apps and the extensive grammar research
that they make us do in all schools

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:03.250
and stuff, which doesn't tend to,
you know, have a great success rate.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:09.550
Uh, those are, those are the,
the key factors, um, that is

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.500
more or less what we found.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.920
Everything else outside of
that is a bit supplemental.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.580
Um, so being able to focus,
being able to concen...

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.570
obviously there's things
like concentration.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.460
You can, I've talked about
meditation with some guys.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:23.750
I know they do it before they study.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.380
And that's something that I'm huge into
deep work, you know, being able to shut

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.800
off your phone, being able to...these,
these sort of things will amplify

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.990
your, your, your ability to learn.

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:35.780
And, uh, they shouldn't
necessarily be taken seriously

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:37.580
because the best guys do do that.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:42.445
And I think, uh, that's, that's something
that can't be taken for granted, you know,

00:13:42.565 --> 00:13:45.595
um, in the whole, in the whole, right.

00:13:45.595 --> 00:13:51.865
And of course the trigger is motivation
and the motivation can be, as you say, it

00:13:51.865 --> 00:13:56.305
can be, you know, focused on one language,
but I don't want to discount the people

00:13:56.305 --> 00:14:00.155
who are just curious about languages
and who study a lot of languages.

00:14:00.735 --> 00:14:04.755
And I, I mean, the stereotype is that
someone from the Heartland of north

00:14:04.755 --> 00:14:08.444
America is not going to be interested
in languages and I can't help but...

00:14:08.745 --> 00:14:12.765
when we talk about people who dabble in
a lot of languages, curious, who work

00:14:12.765 --> 00:14:17.940
curious about a lot of languages, I
can't help, but remember Moses McCormick.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:19.050
I don't know if you're familiar...

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.180
We, sorry to interject.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:24.570
And I, I should just, I just
want to put this in here because

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.190
it's actually kind of tragic.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.330
I mean, obviously we all know
Moses' story, but Moses and I before

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:35.700
obviously Goluremi Language got
started just, uh, just recently Moses

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:38.060
and I had had been in an exchange.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.890
We'd been in a constant exchange
because he was not too far from

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.209
me when I was always in the US.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:46.485
And we continually, we continually
kind of pushed it back.

00:14:46.545 --> 00:14:50.505
We keep, we kept delaying it because
it was just like, oh, I come home every

00:14:50.505 --> 00:14:53.655
six months I come home, you know, I'll,
I'll be home we'll be able to do it and

00:14:53.655 --> 00:14:55.665
we'll do this cool level up together.

00:14:55.875 --> 00:14:58.605
You know, these two African-American
guys, he speaks a lot of Asian languages.

00:14:58.605 --> 00:15:01.065
I speak some Eastern European
languages and it would just blow up the

00:15:01.065 --> 00:15:02.955
internet with this cool, cool thing.

00:15:02.955 --> 00:15:03.825
And we kept delaying it.

00:15:03.825 --> 00:15:04.515
We kept delaying.

00:15:04.545 --> 00:15:06.285
And then I woke up one day
and he had passed away.

00:15:09.705 --> 00:15:10.155
Wow.

00:15:10.245 --> 00:15:11.745
It is extremely tragic.

00:15:12.075 --> 00:15:15.165
And aside from his, see, what, what
impressed me with him was first

00:15:15.165 --> 00:15:17.235
of all, he was a very generous
person, generous with his time.

00:15:18.045 --> 00:15:23.145
And he was, uh, he had a very gentle
storytelling, casual approach,

00:15:23.175 --> 00:15:24.255
which I found very effective.

00:15:24.885 --> 00:15:27.885
But the big thing about him, I
think from a, the point of view of

00:15:27.885 --> 00:15:29.865
language learners is his curiosity.

00:15:30.285 --> 00:15:34.325
So he wasn't learning a language
because he had a girlfriend, can

00:15:34.325 --> 00:15:37.125
I get a job or work for a company.

00:15:37.605 --> 00:15:41.625
He was genuinely curious in languages
and I think successful language learners.

00:15:41.775 --> 00:15:43.365
They have to have curiosity.

00:15:43.695 --> 00:15:47.145
If you aren't curious about another
language, how it works in other

00:15:47.145 --> 00:15:49.275
culture, it's difficult to drum up.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.590
Even if you have a girlfriend
or a boyfriend or whatever it

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:54.180
is, you have to be curious.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.240
And, and the other thing about
him is he comes from, he came

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.640
from a background, like it's not
like he went to a prep school.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.850
No, I mean, I gather, he grew
up, what's known in the states

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.290
as the projects or something.

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:07.890
So it's not like a tremendously
stimulating environment.

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:09.640
And so I don't know what stimula...

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:14.189
what's got him going with, you know, but
obviously you can be in any situation,

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:16.089
if you have that level of curiosity.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:21.270
Uh, and, and you are disciplined enough
to, you know, commit the time necessary.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:24.480
You can learn, focus on one language
and focus on a number of languages,

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.370
but it's got to start with curiousity.

00:16:27.035 --> 00:16:28.275
So I think that's a big part of it.

00:16:28.305 --> 00:16:28.875
For sure.

00:16:28.905 --> 00:16:29.204
Yeah.

00:16:29.235 --> 00:16:31.214
No, I mean, by no means I
would want to deter anyone from

00:16:31.214 --> 00:16:32.145
studying more than one language.

00:16:32.145 --> 00:16:35.865
I mean, I do, I do do that now,
especially this, you know, Moses had

00:16:35.865 --> 00:16:41.324
such an incredible ability to draw
on his knowledge and just to kind of

00:16:41.444 --> 00:16:42.824
get these things to stick for him.

00:16:43.245 --> 00:16:47.474
He may not have had, you know, an in-depth
knowledge of Macedonian for instance, But

00:16:47.474 --> 00:16:48.704
he spent enough time in that, that he...

00:16:49.265 --> 00:16:49.915
He didn't pretend.

00:16:50.114 --> 00:16:51.204
No, by no means.

00:16:51.224 --> 00:16:52.035
And it doesn't matter.

00:16:52.035 --> 00:16:53.925
And I hope that that,
people get that point too.

00:16:53.954 --> 00:16:54.135
Right.

00:16:54.135 --> 00:16:59.354
That's not the point here of, I mean, I
I've hardly rarely have seen any, any,

00:16:59.444 --> 00:17:03.584
any, uh, successful language learner that
says that you have to speak perfectly.

00:17:03.645 --> 00:17:04.875
Otherwise don't do it.

00:17:04.905 --> 00:17:05.474
Don't start.

00:17:05.835 --> 00:17:07.875
And I think a lot of
people have that issue.

00:17:07.875 --> 00:17:11.774
They have that block and it's a, it
stops people from, from speaking or

00:17:11.774 --> 00:17:13.425
from trying and, and stuff like that.

00:17:14.189 --> 00:17:17.310
So, yeah, no, if you're, if your goal
is, I'm just interested in kind of just

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:20.729
connecting with people, because honestly,
if you can just learn two words in another

00:17:20.729 --> 00:17:23.699
language and it's a random language and
you meet these people and you say the

00:17:23.699 --> 00:17:26.219
word it's over, you guys are best friends.

00:17:26.219 --> 00:17:29.250
If you just spoke a little
Swahili to the guy, you know,

00:17:29.429 --> 00:17:30.810
you've, I'm sure you've had that.

00:17:32.545 --> 00:17:32.905
Oh, yeah.

00:17:33.265 --> 00:17:36.745
So what I would suggest now, because
we're also going to do an interview

00:17:36.775 --> 00:17:40.195
here for Will's channel and we're
going to leave a link to Will's

00:17:40.195 --> 00:17:41.695
channel in the description box.

00:17:42.175 --> 00:17:45.475
And in that interview, Will's going
to ask me a bunch of questions.

00:17:45.475 --> 00:17:48.055
I'm not quite sure what, but one
of the things we're going to do is

00:17:48.055 --> 00:17:49.255
to speak in different languages.

00:17:49.465 --> 00:17:53.100
So if you want to hear us speak in
different languages, I suggest that

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:57.210
you mosey on over to Will's channel for
the continuation of this discussion.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:02.830
And we'll just end it here, but thank you
very much for coming on, on my channel.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.770
And we'll be following.

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:05.340
Thanks a lot.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:05.940
Good to be back.

